'''''Dieffenbachia''''', commonly known as '''dumb cane''' or '''leopard lily''', is a [[genus]] of [[tropical]] [[flowering plant]]s  in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Araceae]]. It is native to the New World Tropics from [[Mexico]] and the [[West Indies]] south to [[Argentina]]. Some species are widely cultivated as ornamental plants, especially as houseplants, and have become naturalized on a few tropical islands.{{R|POWO}}<ref name="CROAT">{{cite journal |last1=Croat |first1=Thomas B. |date=December 2004 |title=Revision of ''Dieffenbachia'' (Araceae) of Mexico, Central America, and the West Indies |journal=Annals of the Missouri Botanical Garden |volume=91 |issue=4 |pages=668–772 |jstor=3298554 }}</ref>

''Dieffenbachia'' is a [[perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous]] plant with straight stem, simple and alternate leaves containing white spots and flecks, making it attractive as indoor foliage. [[Species]] in this genus are popular as [[houseplant]]s because of their tolerance of shade. The English names, dumb cane and mother-in-law's tongue (also used for ''[[Sansevieria]]'' species) refer to the poisoning effect of [[raphides]], which can cause temporary inability to speak.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.britannica.com/plant/dumb-cane|title=Dumb cane &#124; plant|website=Encyclopedia Britannica|date=9 July 2024 }}</ref> ''Dieffenbachia'' was named by [[Heinrich Wilhelm Schott]], director of the Botanical Gardens in Vienna, to honor his head gardener Joseph Dieffenbach (1790–1863). 
