==Toxicity==
The [[cell (biology)|cell]]s of the ''Dieffenbachia'' plant contain needle-shaped [[calcium oxalate]] [[crystal]]s called [[raphides]]. If a leaf is chewed, these crystals can cause a temporary burning sensation and [[erythema]].  In rare cases, [[edema]] of tissues exposed to the plant has been reported.  Mastication and ingestion generally result in only mild symptoms.<ref name="J Toxicol Clin Toxicol">{{cite journal |last1=Mrvos |first1=Rita |last2=Dean |first2=Bonnie S. |last3=Krenzelok |first3=Edward P. |title=Philodendron/Dieffenbachia Ingestions: Are They a Problem? |journal=Clinical Toxicology |volume=29 |issue=4 |pages=485–91 |year=1991 |pmid=1749055 |doi=10.3109/15563659109025745 }}</ref> With both children and pets, contact with ''Dieffenbachia'' (typically from chewing) can cause a host of unpleasant symptoms, including intense numbing, oral irritation, excessive drooling, and localized swelling.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/dieffenbachia|title=Dieffenbachia|website=ASPCA}}</ref>  However, these effects are rarely life-threatening. In most cases, symptoms are mild, and can be successfully treated with analgesic agents,<ref name="medscape.com">{{EMedicine|article|1009003|Plant Poisoning, Caladium, Dieffenbachia, and Philodendron}}</ref> antihistamines,<ref>GN Lucas – Sri Lanka Journal of Child Health, 2008 – {{cite web|url=http://www.srilankacollegeofpaediatricians.com/pubs/Microsoft%20Word%20-%20CC%20de%20Silva%20Oration%20Plant%20poisonin.pdf |access-date=March 17, 2009 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110716124034/http://www.srilankacollegeofpaediatricians.com/pubs/Microsoft%20Word%20-%20CC%20de%20Silva%20Oration%20Plant%20poisonin.pdf |archive-date=July 16, 2011 |title=Plant poisoning in Sri Lankan children: A hospital based prospective study }}</ref> or medical charcoal.<ref name="Human 1996">{{cite journal |last1=Lamminpää |first1=Anne |last2=Kinos|first2=Marja |title=Plant poisonings in children |journal=Human & Experimental Toxicology |volume=15 |issue=3 |pages=245–9 |year=1996 |pmid=8839213 |doi=10.1177/096032719601500310 |bibcode=1996HETox..15..245L |s2cid=20090392 }}</ref><ref name="lethal">{{cite journal |last1=Šnajdauf |first1=Jiří |last2=Mixa |first2=Vladimír |last3=Rygl|first3=Michal |last4=Vyhnánek |first4=Martin |last5=Morávek |first5=Jiří |last6=Kabelka |first6=Zdenĕk |title=Aortoesophageal fistula—an unusual complication of esophagitis caused by Dieffenbachia ingestion |journal=Journal of Pediatric Surgery |volume=40 |issue=6 |pages=e29–31 |year=2005 |pmid=15991162 |doi=10.1016/j.jpedsurg.2005.03.036 }}</ref> 

Severe cases can occur if ''Dieffenbachia'' makes prolonged contact with oral mucosal tissue. In such cases, symptoms generally include severe pain which can last for several days to weeks. Hospitalization may be necessary if prolonged contact is made with the throat, in which severe swelling has the potential to affect breathing. 

Gastric evacuation or lavage is seldom indicated.<ref name="Human 1996"/> In patients with exposure to toxic plants, 70% are children younger than 5 years.

Stories that ''Dieffenbachia'' is a deadly poison are [[urban legend]]s.<ref name="snopes.com">{{cite web | url=http://www.snopes.com/household-dieffenbachia-deadly | title=Common Household Plant Dieffenbachia Deadly Poisonous? | date=14 June 2017 | publisher=snopes.com | access-date=17 June 2017}}</ref>

